This sample code is written to verify the properties and behavior of ArrayList's Add, Remove, Insert, Set methods. `PUT_AddContract`, `PUT_RemoveContract`, `PUT_InsertContract`, `PUT_SetContract` are four properties written to cover all possible edge cases and assertions in ArrayList's methods. `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);` will make sure the test cases run no matter the test environment or the state of `arrList` and `x` under test. `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` is used for observing the values for viewing purposes while we debug or troubleshoot the properties' wrong behaviors. The `arrList` object under test is `[PexAssumeUnderTest]`, and it is provided by Pex framework.

Remember to modify the namespace and class name according to your real project. The provided code is an example and will not run directly.

**NOTE:** Testing contracts, especially for methods like `Add()`, `Remove()` and `Set()` that modify the state of an object, requires more attention on how you construct your input parameters. The method under PUT is not necessarily correct without the full context. It's a demonstration of how PEx handles parameters and the verification process and it can help in better understanding of how you can use it to test methods in your projects. But remember to carefully write down the pre-conditions and post-conditions of the PUT methods to ensure robust testing. Testing is a very important part of software development process.

As a best practices, you should try to have the following methods for writing test contracts with PEx:

-   For methods that change the state of the object under test, try to make the input parameters unique and cover all the states of the object

-   Construct a large number of test cases to handle a variety of conditions, including edge conditions and extreme cases

-   For methods that return a result:

    -   Make sure the method returns the expected values under all normal conditions

    -   For methods that may throw exceptions, handle those exceptions properly in your code and ensure that the PEx test cases properly handle exceptions. An exception should be handled appropriately in your code to ensure that you are not introducing additional bugs in your code.

-   Be sure to write down all your assumptions about the problem you are trying to solve

-   You'd have to make assumptions about the internal workings of the method that's being tested, you can't use the 'AssumePrecondition' to change the internal state of the object.

This will ensure to get a robust set of test cases and help in reducing the failure rate of the unit tests. As you'll continue learning unit testing, don't limit yourself with only the provided test classes and examples, try to make your own to suit the specific needs of your project. And remember, having a structured approach in understanding the program's behavior is crucial before writing unit tests. Happy testing~~
